.TH LIBMU2EPROBE 3 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
libmu2eprobe \- parallel TCP reachability probing for the Mu2e DAQ recovery tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <mu2eprobe/probe.hpp>   /* C++ */
.B #include <mu2eprobe/probe.h>     /* C   */
.PP
.B c++ ... \-lmu2eprobe
.PP
.B cc  ... \-lmu2eprobe \-lstdc++
.fi
.PP
The implementation is C++, so a C caller must pull in the C++ runtime as well;
linking with
.BR c++ (1)
does it automatically.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libmu2eprobe
answers one question quickly: of these hosts, which are up? Phase 1 of a
recovery has to answer it for about sixty hosts on three network segments
before it can do anything else, and phase 3 probes a full mesh on the data
network.
.PP
The probe is a non-blocking TCP connect with a bounded
.BR poll (2),
not ICMP. It needs no privilege, it tests something more useful than bare IP
reachability, and TCP/22 is not filtered on the lab network. A
.I refused
connection counts as reachable: the host answered, its sshd simply is not
listening yet, which during a power-on is the normal intermediate state.
.PP
The library is C++17 with a C ABI wrapper and pybind11 bindings. It has no
dependencies beyond the standard library and POSIX sockets, and OpenMP if it is
available \(em without OpenMP the sweep is correct but serial.
.SH C++ INTERFACE
.SS Types
.TP
.B enum class mu2eprobe::Outcome
.BR Open ", " Refused ", " Timeout ", " Unresolved ", " Error .
.TP
.B struct mu2eprobe::Result
.RS
.nf
std::string host;        /* host as given                       */
std::string address;     /* resolved address, empty if unresolved */
std::uint16_t port;
Outcome outcome;
double elapsed_ms;
std::string detail;      /* errno text for Outcome::Error       */
bool reachable() const;  /* Open or Refused                     */
const char* outcome_name() const;
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B struct mu2eprobe::Options
.RS
.nf
std::uint16_t port = 22;
int timeout_ms = 2000;
int threads = 0;         /* 0 = the OpenMP default              */
bool resolve_only = false;
.fi
.RE
.IP
.I port
0 and
.I timeout_ms
at or below 0 select 22 and 2000 rather than being used as written, so a
zero-initialised
.B Options
behaves like a default-constructed one.
.I threads
is ignored entirely when the library was built without OpenMP; the sweep is
then serial and
.B has_openmp()
says so.
.IP
.I resolve_only
stops after
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
and reports a name that resolved as
.BR Outcome::Open .
It is
.I not
evidence the host is up: with this set,
.B reachable()
means "the name resolved".
.SS Functions
.TP
.BI "Result mu2eprobe::probe_one(const std::string& " host ", const Options& " options " = {});"
Probe one host. Never throws; failures are reported in the
.BR Result .
Every address
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
returns for the name is tried in turn, so a host with both an A and a AAAA
record is only
.B Timeout
when neither answered.
.TP
.BI "std::vector<Result> mu2eprobe::probe_many(const std::vector<std::string>& " hosts ", const Options& " options " = {});"
Probe many hosts in parallel. Results are returned
.I in input order
regardless of completion order, because the caller's node list is ordered
meaningfully and should not have to be re-sorted.
.IP
Name resolution is serialised behind a mutex while the connects are not:
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
is not thread-safe on every libc in every configuration, and the resolver
caches each name after the first lookup, so the lock costs little. A sweep of
.I N
hosts therefore costs about one timeout, not
.IR N .
.TP
.BI "std::vector<std::string> mu2eprobe::reachable_hosts(const std::vector<Result>& " results );
The hosts that answered, in input order.
.TP
.B const char* mu2eprobe::version();
.TQ
.B bool mu2eprobe::has_openmp();
Library version, and whether parallelism is actually compiled in.
.SH C INTERFACE
.SS Types
.TP
.B mu2e_probe_options_t
.RS
.nf
uint16_t port;      /* TCP port; 0 selects 22               */
int timeout_ms;     /* per-host budget; 0 selects 2000      */
int threads;        /* 0 = OpenMP default                   */
int resolve_only;   /* non-zero: stop after name resolution */
.fi
.RE
.IP
A
.B NULL
options pointer means the defaults, so a caller with nothing to change need not
build one.
.BR mu2e_probe_default_options ()
fills a struct with the same values for a caller that wants to change one of
them.
.TP
.B mu2e_probe_result_t
.RS
.nf
char host[256];
char address[64];   /* empty when unresolved                */
uint16_t port;
int outcome;        /* MU2E_PROBE_*                         */
double elapsed_ms;
char detail[128];   /* errno text for MU2E_PROBE_ERROR      */
.fi
.RE
.IP
The strings are fixed-size buffers inside the struct, always NUL-terminated,
truncated rather than reallocated if a name or an error text does not fit.
Nothing is ever freed by the caller. There is no
.I reachable
member: test
.I outcome
against
.BR MU2E_PROBE_OPEN " and " MU2E_PROBE_REFUSED ,
which are the two that mean the host answered.
.SS Functions
.nf
.BI "void mu2e_probe_default_options(mu2e_probe_options_t* " options );
.BI "int  mu2e_probe_one(const char* " host ", const mu2e_probe_options_t* " options ,
.BI "                    mu2e_probe_result_t* " out );
.BI "int  mu2e_probe_many(const char* const* " hosts ", size_t " count ,
.BI "                     const mu2e_probe_options_t* " options ,
.BI "                     mu2e_probe_result_t* " results );
.BI "const char* mu2e_probe_outcome_name(int " outcome );
.BI "const char* mu2e_probe_version(void);"
.BI "int  mu2e_probe_has_openmp(void);"
.fi
.PP
.B mu2e_probe_one
returns the outcome code
.RB ( MU2E_PROBE_OPEN ", " MU2E_PROBE_REFUSED ", " MU2E_PROBE_TIMEOUT ,
.BR MU2E_PROBE_UNRESOLVED ", " MU2E_PROBE_ERROR ,
numerically 0 to 4) and fills
.IR out ,
which may be
.BR NULL .
A
.B NULL
.I host
is
.B MU2E_PROBE_ERROR
and fills nothing.
.PP
.B mu2e_probe_many
returns the number of hosts that answered, or
.B \-1
when
.I hosts
or
.I results
is
.BR NULL .
The caller supplies the
.I results
array and it must hold at least
.I count
entries; there is no way to ask how many were written, so size it from
.I count
rather than from the return value. A
.B NULL
entry inside
.I hosts
is
.I not
skipped: it is passed to
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
as an empty name, which most resolvers map to the loopback address, so it is
probed as the local machine and can come back
.B refused
and be counted as reachable. Filter empty names before calling.
.PP
.BR mu2e_probe_outcome_name ()
returns
.BR open ", " refused ", " timeout ", " unresolved " or " error ,
and
.B error
for any code it does not recognise. These are the same strings
.B Result::outcome_name()
returns, which is what makes the C and C++ output comparable.
.PP
All functions are safe to call from multiple threads.
.B mu2e_probe_many
runs its own parallelism internally, so there is no reason to call it from
several threads at once.
.SH PYTHON INTERFACE
When the pybind11 extension is built:
.RS
.EX
import mu2eprobe

results = mu2eprobe.probe_many(hosts, port=22, timeout_ms=2000, threads=0)
one     = mu2eprobe.probe_one(host, port=22, timeout_ms=2000)
up      = mu2eprobe.reachable_hosts(results)
mu2eprobe.has_openmp()
mu2eprobe.__version__
.EE
.RE
.PP
A
.B Result
carries
.IR host ", " address ", " port ", " outcome ", " elapsed_ms " and " detail ,
plus
.IR reachable " and " outcome_name ,
which are
.I properties
here although
.B outcome_name()
is a method in C++.
.I outcome
is a
.B mu2eprobe.Outcome
.RB ( OPEN ", " REFUSED ", " TIMEOUT ", " UNRESOLVED ", " ERROR ),
not the string.
.PP
Two limitations of the binding, not of the library:
.B Options
is exposed but no module function accepts one \(em the sweeps take keyword
arguments \(em and there is consequently no way to reach
.I resolve_only
from Python. There is no
.B version()
function either; the version is
.BR __version__ .
.PP
The GIL is released for the duration of a sweep, which is the point of the
extension. The recovery tools do not import this directly \(em they use
.IR mu2edaq_power_recovery.sweep ,
which uses the extension when it is present and a thread-pool implementation
over
.BR socket (7)
when it is not, so a missing extension changes the speed of a sweep and nothing
else.
.I sweep.BACKEND
names the one in use, and the report records it, so a timing difference between
two runs has an explanation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
#include <mu2eprobe/probe.hpp>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
  mu2eprobe::Options options;
  options.timeout_ms = 1000;
  const auto results = mu2eprobe::probe_many(
      {"mu2e-trk-01.fnal.gov", "mu2e-trk-02.fnal.gov"}, options);
  for (const auto& r : results)
    std::printf("%-30s %s\\n", r.host.c_str(), r.outcome_name());
}
.EE
.RE
.SH BUILDING
.RS
.EX
cmake \-S . \-B build \-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake \-\-build build
ctest \-\-test\-dir build \-\-output\-on\-failure
cmake \-\-install build \-\-prefix /usr/local
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B \-DBUILD_BINDINGS=OFF
skips the Python extension;
.B \-DBUILD_PROBE=OFF
skips both the library and
.BR mu2e-probe (1).
Neither is on the critical path for a recovery: the Python fallback covers the
sweep either way.
.PP
The library builds static by default, as CMake does;
.B \-DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON
gives a shared one, versioned from the project version. OpenMP is looked for
with
.B find_package(OpenMP QUIET)
and its absence is a configure-time message, not an error \(em check the
.B mu2eprobe:
line in the cmake output, or call
.BR has_openmp ()
at run time.
